# Showcase notes, chapters 2 to 18. Drafted for Wisdom, reviewed: false until the owner checks REVIEW.md.
SHOW = {
'2.1': dict(
 notice="The student's first words after the teaching are a laugh at himself: all this time.",
 words=[("aho", "how strange, ah"), ("nirañjanaḥ", "spotless"), ("śāntaḥ", "at peace"), ("bodhaḥ", "awareness"), ("aham", "I am"), ("prakṛteḥ paraḥ", "beyond nature"), ("etāvantam kālam", "all this time"), ("mohena", "by delusion"), ("eva", "only"), ("viḍambitaḥ", "fooled, made a mockery of")],
 commentary="Chapter 2 belongs to Janaka. He has heard nineteen verses from his teacher and something has turned over. He begins with aho, an exclamation of wonder that runs through the whole chapter. Then he says what he is: spotless, at peace, awareness, beyond nature. Prakṛti, nature, includes body and mind, so he is placing himself beyond both. The second line has a wry tone. Viḍambita means mocked or made a fool of. For all these years, he says, I have been fooled by delusion. The Advaita tradition values this verse as a picture of what recognition feels like from inside. There is no pride in it and no drama, only surprise that something so plain was missed for so long. Many people know a small version of this, the moment when a family quarrel that felt enormous suddenly looks like a misunderstanding. The rest of chapter 2 is Janaka testing the new view against everything he knows, and finding that it holds.",
 bridge=(18, 73, 'same', "Arjuna, at the end of the Gita, says his delusion is gone and his memory of who he is has returned."),
),
'2.10': dict(
 notice="Three pairs, and each ornament or form dissolves back into what it was made of.",
 words=[("mattaḥ", "from me"), ("vinirgatam", "come out"), ("viśvam", "the world"), ("mayi eva", "into me alone"), ("layam eṣyati", "will dissolve"), ("mṛdi", "into clay"), ("kumbhaḥ", "a pot"), ("jale", "into water"), ("vīciḥ", "a wave"), ("kanake", "into gold"), ("kaṭakam", "a bracelet")],
 commentary="Janaka gives three examples, each a form and its material. A pot is clay given a shape. A wave is water in motion. A bracelet, kaṭaka, is gold bent into a ring. In each case the form has a name and a use, and in each case it returns to its material without any loss to the material. The clay was never less clay for being a pot. Janaka says the world comes out of him and returns into him in the same way. Advaita uses these examples, which go back to the Chandogya Upanishad's teaching that all clay things are only clay, to explain how one reality can appear as many things. The name and shape are real for use. You drink from the pot and wear the bracelet. But what they are made of is what they are. Applied to the self, the verse removes a fear. If you are the gold, the melting of a bracelet is a change of shape and not an ending.",
 bridge=(9, 7, 'same', "Krishna says all beings return into his nature at the end of an age and come forth again at its start."),
),
'2.25': dict(
 notice="Living beings are waves that rise, collide, play and sink, by their own nature.",
 words=[("mayi", "in me"), ("ananta-mahā-ambhodhau", "in the endless great ocean"), ("āścaryam", "wonder"), ("jīva-vīcayaḥ", "waves of living beings"), ("udyanti", "rise"), ("ghnanti", "strike each other"), ("khelanti", "play"), ("praviśanti", "enter back, sink"), ("svabhāvataḥ", "by their own nature")],
 commentary="Chapter 2 ends with the ocean. Janaka sees himself as an endless ocean and living beings as its waves. The four verbs are worth reading slowly: they rise, they strike each other, they play, they sink back. That is a fair summary of any life, and of all lives together. The word ghnanti, they strike or even kill, is not softened. Waves crash into each other. People hurt each other. The verse does not pretend otherwise. What it adds is svabhāvataḥ, by their own nature. The waves do this because that is what waves do, and the ocean is not damaged by any of it. The Advaita reading is that this is how the knower looks at the world, with wonder, āścarya, and without being thrown about. The knower still feels for those who suffer, and sees their suffering from a depth it does not reach. The ocean returns in chapters 6, 7 and 15, each time with a slightly different lesson.",
 bridge=(2, 70, 'same', "Krishna compares the one at peace to the ocean, which stays still while rivers keep pouring in."),
),
'6.2': dict(
 notice="The same refrain closes all four verses of chapter 6: nothing to give up, take or dissolve.",
 words=[("mahā-udadhiḥ iva", "like the great ocean"), ("aham", "I"), ("saḥ", "that"), ("prapañcaḥ", "the spread-out world of many things"), ("vīci-sannibhaḥ", "like a wave"), ("iti", "thus"), ("jñānam", "knowledge"), ("tathā", "so"), ("etasya", "of this"), ("na tyāgaḥ", "no giving up"), ("na grahaḥ", "no taking"), ("layaḥ", "dissolving")],
 commentary="In chapter 5 Ashtavakra told Janaka four times to go to rest, layaṃ vraja, to dissolve. In chapter 6 the reply, in four verses, is that there is nothing to dissolve. This verse gives the ocean image. I am like the great ocean, and the world of many things is like a wave. Knowing this, there is no giving up, no taking and no dissolving. The refrain is the point. If the wave is water, you do not need to throw it away to reach the water, and you do not need to grab it either. Even dissolving it is unnecessary, since it is already nothing other than ocean. The Advaita teachers see chapter 6 as a refinement. Renunciation and absorption are useful ideas on the way, but they still assume a world standing apart from the Self. Once that assumption is gone, these efforts have no object. For a reader, the verse is an invitation to stop managing. Some days the waves are high. You are still the ocean.",
 bridge=None,
),
'7.1': dict(
 notice="The ship of the world is moved by its own wind, and the ocean does not mind.",
 words=[("mayi", "in me"), ("ananta-mahā-ambhodhau", "in the endless great ocean"), ("viśva-potaḥ", "the ship of the world"), ("itastataḥ", "here and there"), ("bhramati", "drifts, wanders"), ("sva-anta-vātena", "by the wind of its own mind"), ("na", "not"), ("mama", "for me"), ("asti", "there is"), ("asahiṣṇutā", "impatience, being troubled")],
 commentary="In chapter 2 the ship of the world sank when the wind of the mind fell still. Here, in chapter 7, Janaka watches the ship drift here and there, pushed by its own wind, and says he feels no impatience about it. Asahiṣṇutā is the inability to bear something. Its absence is a calm tolerance, the kind a deep sea has toward the boats on its surface. The phrase svāntavātena can be read as by its own inner wind, the mind of each being. The world moves according to the minds within it. The ocean does not steer the ship. The Advaita reading draws a practical lesson. Much of our distress comes from wanting the world to move differently, and from feeling that it is our job to steer all of it. The knower still acts where action is his to take, and is not tormented by the drifting of everything else. Chapter 7 has five verses in this voice, each one Janaka settling more fully into the view his teacher gave him.",
 bridge=None,
),
'8.1': dict(
 notice="Six movements of the mind are listed, and each one is called bondage.",
 words=[("tadā", "then"), ("bandhaḥ", "bondage"), ("yadā", "when"), ("cittam", "the mind"), ("kiñcit", "something"), ("vāñchati", "wants"), ("śocati", "grieves"), ("muñcati", "lets go"), ("gṛhṇāti", "grabs"), ("hṛṣyati", "is delighted"), ("kupyati", "is angry")],
 commentary="Chapter 8 is four verses long and gives the clearest definition of bondage and freedom in the text. This first verse lists six movements: wanting, grieving, letting go, grabbing, delight and anger. The word kiñcit, something, repeats through the line. Bondage happens when the mind does any of these about something. Notice that letting go is on the list. Even renouncing something, if the mind grips the act of renouncing, is bondage. The Advaita commentators read the verse as a description of where bondage lives. Objects are neutral and the Self is free, so bondage can only live in the mind's reaching and recoiling. That makes the verse usable. The next time you feel pulled by a want or a grievance, you can name it: this is the movement Ashtavakra called bondage. Naming it will not end it. It does show you where it lives. The following verse gives the mirror image, the same six movements absent, and calls that freedom.",
 bridge=(2, 62, 'same', "Krishna traces how dwelling on objects breeds attachment, then desire, then anger."),
),
'8.4': dict(
 notice="The word helayā, lightly or playfully, sets the tone for the whole instruction.",
 words=[("yadā", "when"), ("na aham", "there is no 'I'"), ("tadā", "then"), ("mokṣaḥ", "freedom"), ("aham", "'I'"), ("bandhanam", "bondage"), ("matvā", "having understood"), ("iti", "this"), ("helayā", "lightly, playfully"), ("kiñcit", "anything"), ("mā gṛhāṇa", "do not grab"), ("vimuñca mā", "do not drop")],
 commentary="The first line is as short as Sanskrit allows. When there is no I, there is freedom. When there is I, there is bondage. Aham here means the ego-sense, the small self that claims things, and not the Self of the earlier chapters. Then comes the instruction, and its most important word is helayā. It means lightly, easily, as if in play. Having seen this, says Ashtavakra, do not grab anything and do not drop anything, and do it lightly. The tradition reads helayā as a guard against turning the teaching into another strain. If you fight the ego with the ego, you have doubled it. If you try hard to neither grasp nor reject, you are grasping at non-grasping. The light touch is the method. It resembles holding a bird: too tight and you hurt it, too loose and it goes. With this verse the four-verse chapter ends, having said in four verses what some texts take volumes to say.",
 bridge=(2, 71, 'same', "Krishna says peace comes to one who lives without craving, without 'mine' and without 'I'."),
),
'9.6': dict(
 notice="The verse asks a question and expects the answer yes: such a person is a guru.",
 words=[("kṛtvā", "having gained"), ("mūrti-parijñānam", "full knowledge of the nature"), ("caitanyasya", "of awareness"), ("na kim", "is he not"), ("guruḥ", "a guru, a teacher"), ("nirveda", "weariness with the world"), ("samatā", "evenness of mind"), ("yuktyā", "by reasoning"), ("yaḥ", "who"), ("tārayati", "carries across"), ("saṃsṛteḥ", "from the round of birth and death")],
 commentary="Chapter 9 is often titled nirveda, weariness with the world, and this verse explains how that weariness can become freedom. Three means are named: nirveda itself, samatā or evenness of mind, and yukti, reasoning. Through them a person comes to know the nature of awareness, mūrti meaning its true form. Such a person tārayati, carries across the river of saṃsāra. The verb can mean crossing oneself or ferrying others, and the commentators allow both. Then the question: is such a person not a guru? The verse widens the idea of a teacher. A guru here is defined by what they have understood and by their ability to cross. Title and lineage are not mentioned. This fits the story of the text, in which a boy with a bent body teaches a king. People searching 'Ashtavakra Gita 9.6' sometimes land on Bhagavad Gita 9.6, which is a different verse about all beings resting in God as the wind rests in space. The two texts agree on the role of the teacher.",
 bridge=(4, 34, 'same', "Krishna says the wise who have seen the truth will teach you, if you approach them with humility and questions."),
),
'11.5': dict(
 notice="The verse names one source of sorrow, worry, and adds 'and nothing else here'.",
 words=[("cintayā", "by worry, anxious thought"), ("jāyate", "is born"), ("duḥkham", "sorrow"), ("na anyathā", "not otherwise"), ("iha", "here"), ("iti", "thus"), ("niścayī", "one who is sure"), ("tayā hīnaḥ", "free of it"), ("sukhī", "happy"), ("śāntaḥ", "at peace"), ("sarvatra", "everywhere"), ("galita-spṛhaḥ", "with wanting melted away")],
 commentary="Chapter 11 has eight verses, each built around niścayī, one who is certain. This one names worry, cintā, as the source of sorrow, and says nānyatheha, not otherwise, here. The claim is strong. Events happen, losses happen, but the sorrow that lingers and spreads is made of thought turning over and over. Someone waiting for a medical result knows this: the hours of waiting often hurt more than the news. The Advaita commentators do not tell the reader to stop thinking by force, which rarely works. They point to certainty. One who is sure of this, who has seen it in his own experience, is freed of worry by that seeing. Then three results follow, as in many verses of this text: happy, peaceful, and with wanting melted, galita-spṛha. The image of melting is gentle. Desire is left to soften and run off once the heat of worry is gone. The verse is a good one to read slowly, more than once, on an anxious evening.",
 bridge=(2, 66, 'same', "Krishna asks how there can be happiness for one who has no peace."),
),
'15.4': dict(
 notice="The verse begins with denials and ends with a walk: move about at ease.",
 words=[("na tvam dehaḥ", "you are not the body"), ("na te dehaḥ", "the body is not yours"), ("bhoktā", "enjoyer"), ("kartā", "doer"), ("bhavān", "you"), ("cidrūpaḥ", "awareness itself"), ("asi", "you are"), ("sadā", "always"), ("sākṣī", "witness"), ("nirapekṣaḥ", "needing nothing"), ("sukham", "at ease"), ("cara", "move about, live")],
 commentary="Chapter 15 returns to the themes of chapter 1, and this verse gathers the main points of chapter 1 into one line. You are not the body, and the body is not yours. You are neither doer nor enjoyer. You are awareness, always the witness, needing nothing. Then the instruction: sukhaṃ cara, move about at ease. Cara is a verb of walking, wandering, living one's life. It is a fitting end. The teaching sends Janaka back into his day with a lighter step, with no demand that he sit still forever. The phrase na te deha, the body is not yours, goes a step beyond na tvaṃ deha, you are not the body. Even ownership is denied. The Advaita reading is that the body belongs to nature and is used by awareness, the way a road is used by a traveller. The traveller does not own it and is not harmed when it ends. Readers who want one verse to memorise from the Ashtavakra Gita often choose this one.",
 bridge=(2, 20, 'same', "Krishna says the Self is never born and never dies, and is not slain when the body is slain."),
),
'15.7': dict(
 notice="The line about waves on the sea, first used in 3.3 as a question, returns here as a plain statement.",
 words=[("viśvam", "the world"), ("sphurati", "shimmers, flashes into view"), ("yatra", "in which"), ("idam", "this"), ("taraṅgāḥ iva", "like waves"), ("sāgare", "on the sea"), ("tat", "that"), ("tvam eva", "you are"), ("na sandehaḥ", "there is no doubt"), ("cinmūrte", "O form of awareness"), ("vijvaraḥ", "free of fever"), ("bhava", "be")],
 commentary="In chapter 3 Ashtavakra used the first half of this verse to scold Janaka: you know you are that in which the world rises like waves, so why do you run about like a pauper? Here, in chapter 15, the same half-line comes back without the scolding. That in which the world shimmers like waves on the sea: you are that, without doubt. Sphurati is a lovely verb. It means to flash, to quiver into view, the way light catches on moving water. The world is pictured as that kind of flicker on the surface of awareness. Then Ashtavakra calls Janaka cinmūrti, form of awareness, and tells him to be vijvara, free of fever. Jvara is the ordinary word for fever, and it is used for the restless heat of craving and worry. The Advaita reading treats the verse as close to the great Upanishadic sayings, such as tat tvam asi, you are that. It is spoken with confidence. Na sandehaḥ, there is no doubt.",
 bridge=None,
),
'15.8': dict(
 notice="Ashtavakra repeats one word, śraddhasva, trust, as a father would.",
 words=[("śraddhasva", "trust, have faith"), ("tāta", "my child"), ("na atra", "not here"), ("moham", "confusion"), ("kuruṣva", "make, fall into"), ("bhoḥ", "dear one"), ("jñāna-svarūpaḥ", "whose nature is knowledge"), ("bhagavān", "the Lord"), ("ātmā", "the Self"), ("tvam", "you"), ("prakṛteḥ paraḥ", "beyond nature")],
 commentary="This verse has a tenderness rare in the text. Ashtavakra says śraddhasva twice, trust, trust, and calls Janaka tāta, child, and bhoḥ, an affectionate address. Śraddhā in the Vedanta tradition is not blind belief. The Vivekachudamani, traditionally attributed to Shankara, defines it as holding the words of the teacher and the scripture to be true, a working confidence that lets you test what you hear. Without it nothing can be learned, since you will not stay with a teaching long enough to see whether it is true. The content of what is to be trusted is large. You are the Self, whose nature is knowledge, the Lord, beyond nature. Bhagavān is a word usually reserved for God. Ashtavakra uses it for Janaka's own Self without hesitation. The Advaita teachers say this is where knowledge and devotion meet: the Lord you worship and the Self you are turn out to be one. The verse asks the reader for something modest, which is to stop arguing with the teaching long enough to look.",
 bridge=(4, 39, 'same', "Krishna says the one with trust, devoted and self-controlled, gains knowledge and soon reaches peace."),
),
'15.11': dict(
 notice="This is Janaka's own line from 7.2, given back to him by his teacher with 'I' changed to 'you'.",
 words=[("tvayi", "in you"), ("ananta-mahā-ambhodhau", "in the endless great ocean"), ("viśva-vīciḥ", "the wave of the world"), ("svabhāvataḥ", "by its own nature"), ("udetu", "let it rise"), ("vā", "or"), ("astam āyātu", "let it sink"), ("na te", "not for you"), ("vṛddhiḥ", "growth"), ("na vā kṣatiḥ", "nor loss")],
 commentary="In chapter 7 Janaka said: in me, the endless ocean, the wave of the world rises or sinks by its own nature, and I neither grow nor shrink. Here Ashtavakra says the same line with one change. In you. The teacher hands the student's insight back to him, confirmed. The verb forms matter. Udetu and astam āyātu are imperatives of permission: let it rise, let it set. There is no command to stop the wave or to ride it, only permission for it to do what it does. Then: na te vṛddhir na vā kṣatiḥ, there is no growth and no loss for you. Advaita teachers use this verse to describe a calm that does not depend on outcomes. A good year and a bad year are both waves. The ocean is not richer after a high tide. Someone who has watched savings go up and down, or a career rise and stall, can test the verse against that experience and see whether the one watching ever changed.",
 bridge=(2, 70, 'same', "Krishna describes the ocean that stays unmoved while rivers pour into it, and the person at peace like it."),
),
'15.14': dict(
 notice="Bracelets, armlets and anklets are three shapes, and there is only one gold.",
 words=[("yat", "whatever"), ("tvam", "you"), ("paśyasi", "see"), ("tatra", "there"), ("ekaḥ", "alone"), ("tvam eva", "you yourself"), ("pratibhāsase", "appear"), ("kim", "does"), ("pṛthak", "apart"), ("bhāsate", "appear"), ("svarṇāt", "from gold"), ("kaṭaka", "bracelet"), ("aṅgada", "armlet"), ("nūpuram", "anklet")],
 commentary="Indian households have long kept wealth as jewellery, and every family knows that a bracelet can be melted into an anklet without losing a gram of gold. Ashtavakra uses this everyday knowledge. Whatever you see, you alone appear there. Do the bracelet, the armlet and the anklet appear as something apart from the gold? Of course not. You may admire the shape, wear it on a different part of the body, call it by a different name, and the jeweller still weighs it as gold. The Advaita reading applies this to all experience. Trees, cities and people are different shapes, and what appears as all of them is awareness. The world keeps its variety, and a good jeweller cares a great deal about the shape. Beneath every difference there is a unity that is never lost. The example appeared in 2.10 with a single bracelet. Here three ornaments make the point that variety itself is no obstacle to oneness.",
 bridge=(7, 19, 'same', "Krishna says the rare great soul is the one who sees that Vasudeva is all."),
),
'16.11': dict(
 notice="Even the three highest gods as teachers would not be enough without forgetting.",
 words=[("haraḥ", "Shiva"), ("yadi", "if"), ("upadeṣṭā", "teacher"), ("te", "your"), ("hariḥ", "Vishnu"), ("kamalajaḥ", "lotus-born, Brahma"), ("api vā", "or even"), ("tathā api", "even so"), ("na", "not"), ("tava", "your"), ("svāsthyam", "ease, resting in yourself"), ("sarva-vismaraṇāt ṛte", "without forgetting everything")],
 commentary="Chapter 16 opens and closes with the same refrain: you will not be at ease until you forget everything. In this last verse Ashtavakra raises the stakes. Even if Shiva, Vishnu or Brahma were your teacher, it would not be enough. The word for ease is svāsthya, literally standing in oneself, the same root as the modern Hindi word for health. What is to be forgotten is everything learned, believed and held as a position. The Advaita reading is careful here. Ashtavakra quotes scripture and relies on it, and he names the gods with respect. His point is that knowledge held as information keeps you outside of what it describes. A person who has memorised every map of a city still has to put the maps away to walk its streets. The verse is useful for anyone who reads a great deal about spiritual matters, this page included. At some point the reading has to stop and the resting has to begin.",
 bridge=(2, 52, 'same', "Krishna says that when your understanding crosses the thicket of confusion, you will grow indifferent to all you have heard and will hear."),
),
'17.3': dict(
 notice="An elephant that loves sallaki leaves will not touch bitter neem.",
 words=[("na jātu", "never"), ("viṣayāḥ", "objects of the senses"), ("ke api", "any"), ("svārāmam", "one who delights in the Self"), ("harṣayanti", "please"), ("amī", "these"), ("sallakī-pallava", "sallaki leaves, a tree elephants love"), ("prītam", "delighted by"), ("iva", "like"), ("ibham", "an elephant"), ("nimba-pallavāḥ", "neem leaves")],
 commentary="Sallaki is the Indian frankincense tree, Boswellia, whose leaves elephants love. Neem leaves are bitter. An elephant that has its fill of sallaki will not bother with neem. Ashtavakra says sense objects are like neem to one who delights in the Self, svārāma. They are simply not interesting. The image avoids two common mistakes. It does not say the knower fights his desires, and it does not say objects are evil. Neem is a useful tree. It just has nothing to offer an elephant that has better food. The Advaita teachers read this as the natural end of desire, which comes from tasting something more satisfying. The Bhagavad Gita says the same thing in its second chapter: objects fall away from one who fasts, but the taste for them remains until the highest is seen. People searching for 'Ashtavakra Gita 17.3' often land on Bhagavad Gita 17.3, which is about faith shaping a person. This is the verse they were looking for.",
 bridge=(2, 59, 'same', "Krishna says even the taste for objects falls away once the highest has been seen."),
),
'18.1': dict(
 notice="The hundred-verse chapter opens with a bow, before any argument.",
 words=[("yasya", "whose"), ("bodha-udaye", "at the dawning of awareness"), ("tāvat", "then"), ("svapnavat", "like a dream"), ("bhavati", "becomes"), ("bhramaḥ", "error, the mistaken world"), ("tasmai", "to that"), ("sukha-eka-rūpāya", "whose one form is joy"), ("namaḥ", "I bow"), ("śāntāya", "to the peaceful"), ("tejase", "to the light")],
 commentary="Chapter 18 is a hundred verses long, a third of the whole text, and it is often titled the chapter of peace. It opens with a salutation, namaḥ. What is saluted has no name: it is that light, tejas, that peace, śānta, whose one form is joy, at whose dawning the error of the world becomes like a dream. Dreams are the tradition's favourite evidence. While dreaming, the dream world is fully real, with its fears and pleasures. On waking, nothing of it needs to be destroyed. It simply stops being taken as real. The verse says the same happens to the world when awareness dawns. The Advaita commentators note that the bow is directed at what Ashtavakra has been teaching all along, the Self, so the teacher bows to the student's own nature before speaking. A reader can use the verse the same way, as a short act of respect at the start of a day, directed at the awareness that is about to see it.",
 bridge=(2, 69, 'same', "Krishna says what is night for all beings is where the self-controlled one is awake."),
),
'18.35': dict(
 notice="Six qualities of the Self are listed, and then the surprise: those devoted to practice do not know it.",
 words=[("śuddham", "pure"), ("buddham", "awake"), ("priyam", "dear, beloved"), ("pūrṇam", "whole"), ("niṣprapañcam", "beyond the spread of the world"), ("nirāmayam", "untouched by sorrow or sickness"), ("ātmānam", "the Self"), ("tam", "that"), ("na jānanti", "do not know"), ("tatra", "there"), ("abhyāsa-parāḥ", "devoted to practice"), ("janāḥ", "people")],
 commentary="The first line is a portrait of the Self in six words. Pure, awake, dear, whole, beyond the spread of the world, free of sickness. Priya, dear, is a striking choice. The Upanishads say that everything is loved for the sake of the Self, and here the Self itself is called the beloved. Then the second line turns: people devoted to practice do not know it. This is the same point made in 1.15 and repeated through chapter 18. If you practise in order to reach the Self, you have placed it somewhere else, at the end of the practice. Advaita teachers read the verse as aimed at a particular trap, where years of discipline become a way of postponing recognition. They do not read it as a ban on practice for everyone. The Bhagavad Gita takes a different emphasis and tells Arjuna that the restless mind is steadied by practice and dispassion. Many readers find both true at different stages. People searching for 'Ashtavakra samhita 18.35' are looking for this verse.",
 bridge=(6, 35, 'contrast', "Krishna tells Arjuna the restless mind is steadied by practice and dispassion, the step Ashtavakra treats as already behind his student."),
),
'18.59': dict(
 notice="The word sukham, at ease, appears six times in two lines.",
 words=[("sukham", "at ease, happily"), ("āste", "sits"), ("śete", "sleeps"), ("āyāti", "comes"), ("yāti", "goes"), ("vakti", "speaks"), ("bhuṅkte", "eats"), ("vyavahāre api", "even in everyday dealings"), ("śānta-dhīḥ", "one whose mind is calm")],
 commentary="After many verses of paradox and negation, chapter 18 offers this picture of ordinary life. At ease he sits, at ease he sleeps, at ease he comes and goes, at ease he speaks, at ease he eats. The word sukham opens each phrase, and the repetition is the meaning: the same ease runs through every activity. The last phrase is vyavahāre 'pi, even in vyavahāra, which means business, transactions, the give and take of daily life. The calm-minded person needs no quiet cave. He stays calm while paying bills and talking to neighbours. The Advaita reading treats this as the mark of a jīvanmukta, one free while living. Freedom shows itself in how a person eats lunch and answers questions. A reader can use the verse as a check during an ordinary day. Sitting, walking, speaking, eating: is there ease in it? The verse does not ask for different activities, only for a different quality in the same ones.",
 bridge=(5, 8, 'same', "Krishna says the one who knows the truth thinks 'I do nothing' while seeing, hearing, walking, sleeping and breathing."),
),
'18.100': dict(
 notice="The last verse of Ashtavakra's teaching refuses both the crowd and the forest.",
 words=[("na dhāvati", "does not run"), ("jana-ākīrṇam", "to the crowd, the busy town"), ("na araṇyam", "nor to the forest"), ("upaśānta-dhīḥ", "one whose mind is at peace"), ("yathā tathā", "however it is"), ("yatra tatra", "wherever it is"), ("samaḥ eva", "the same"), ("avatiṣṭhate", "remains")],
 commentary="This is Ashtavakra's last word in the text. Chapters 19 and 20 belong to Janaka. Ashtavakra ends with a picture of where the peaceful mind lives, and the answer is nowhere in particular. It does not run to the crowded town and it does not run to the forest. Both directions were respected paths in old India. Some people sought engagement, others withdrew to hermitages. Ashtavakra declines both as escapes. Yathā tathā, however things are. Yatra tatra, wherever one is. The mind stays the same. The Advaita commentators read this as the fruit of the whole teaching. A person who knows the Self has no need to arrange circumstances, since peace does not come from them. For a reader, the verse removes a common excuse. Many of us think we would be calm somewhere else, after the move, after the project, away from the noise. Ashtavakra says the peaceful mind runs to neither. It stays where it is and is at peace there.",
 bridge=(12, 18, 'same', "Krishna describes the one who is the same to friend and foe, in honour and dishonour, in heat and cold."),
),
}
